1. Workspace Definition A new workspace is created with the name "Reuse Models" and the description "Demonstrate Model Reuse."

2. The Model (Defining the building blocks) This section defines the static elements of the architecture and their relationships.

Global Elements: Two primary elements are defined that will be reused:

A Person named "Customer".

A Software System named "Website".

Scoped Systems and Relationships: Two separate software system "containers" are defined to establish different contexts (scopes) for relationships:

Context 1 (system1): A software system named "system1" is defined. Inside this system's scope, a specific relationship exists:

The Customer uses the Website. This relationship is labeled: "Relationship scoped to system1".

Context 2 (system2): Another software system named "system2" is defined. Inside this system's scope, a different relationship exists:

The Website interacts with the Customer. This relationship is labeled: "Relationship scoped to system2".

3. The Views (Defining the diagrams) This section defines how the model is visualized. It creates two distinct diagrams to illustrate the different scopes defined above.

Diagram 1: systemContext system1

This diagram focuses on the context of "system1".

It explicitly includes the Customer and the Website.

Crucially, it explicitly excludes the relationship belonging to the other system (rel2, where the Website calls the Customer). By default, it will only show the relationship scoped to system1.

The layout is generated automatically.

Diagram 2: systemContext system2

This diagram focuses on the context of "system2".

It also explicitly includes the Customer and the Website.

Crucially, it explicitly excludes the relationship belonging to the first system (rel1, where the Customer calls the Website). It will only show the relationship scoped to system2.

The layout is generated automatically.













